at is correlated to the outer query. In the SELECT clause of the outer query, include a CASE statement or similar logic to handle selection from the subquery result. - Example Transformation: SELECT left_table.*, right_table.* FROM left_table LEFT JOIN right_table ON left_table.id = right_table.foreign_id Becomes: SELECT left_table.*, COALESCE(subquery.alias1, \'default\') FROM left_table LEFT OUTER JOIN (SELECT foreign_id, column1 AS alias1 FROM right_table) subquery ON left_table.id = subquery.foreign_id"""\n\nRule Subset Sequences:\n### Rule Sequence 1: ["AGGREGATE_PROJECT_MERGE"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 2: ["FILTER_SUB_QUERY_TO_CORRELATE", "JOIN_TO_CORRELATE"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 3: ["FILTER_INTO_JOIN"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 4: ["FILTER_INTO_JOIN"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 5: ["AGGREGATE_PROJECT_MERGE"]'}], 'response': 'Here\'s the reasoning and the arranged rule names:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using three different strategies. The first strategy transforms the scalar sub-query into a LEFT JOIN operation with an aggregate function on the column(s) being selected in the sub-query, simplifying complex queries by applying filter conditions on the result set of an INNER JOIN operation. The second strategy applies Case 1 of the SQL query rewrite rule, which recommends moving filter conditions into the ON clause of the INNER JOIN or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side. The third strategy simplifies the original complex query by directly applying aggregate functions and GROUP BY to the data source, eliminating the outer projection layer if it only renames columns without transforming them, and removing the inner SELECT layer.\n\n```python\n["FILTER_SUB_QUERY_TO_CORRELATE", "FILTER_INTO_JOIN", "AGGREGATE_PROJECT_MERGE"]\n```\n\nThis sequence is based on Rule Sequence 2: ["FILTER_SUB_QUERY_TO_CORRELATE", "JOIN_TO_CORRELATE"].', 'time': 2.3573977947235107}
